language-derived-from-the-record-8 = unlines (cons "**6. Fluency and theorem-grade sentences.** A word's meaning for a reader is the family of what followed the past occurrences of the word in the reader's record that the present context selects, transported to now; it is determinate when that family is contractible, and shared between speakers exactly as far as their records are joined along shared and copying events. A fluent speaker's record holds each word's occurrences joined into the human superorganism's copying graph back to its source. A sentence is theorem-grade when, for every fluent reader, the selected family of every word in it is contractible: the sentence has one meaning and can be evaluated rationally. A speaker without fluency selects over a small, poorly joined record; their utterance has determinate meaning only by descent through the records of others: it means what fluent speakers' selections make it mean. Their meaning rests on those who resolved the words." nil)
